    deepExplanation: "useLayoutEffect runs after React has committed DOM changes but before the browser paints. It is appropriate when an effect must measure or synchronously adjust layout to avoid visible flicker, such as positioning a tooltip. It should not be used for ordinary data fetching or non-visual synchronization because it can block painting.\\n\\n```tsx\nimport { useLayoutEffect, useRef, useState } from \"react\";\n\nexport function Tooltip({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {\n  const ref = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const [height, setHeight] = useState(0);\n\n  useLayoutEffect(() => {\n    if (ref.current) setHeight(ref.current.getBoundingClientRect().height);\n  }, []);\n\n  return <div ref={ref} style={{ transform: `translateY(-${height}px)` }}>{children}</div>;\n}\n```\\n\\nWhy it matters: This topic affects correctness, maintainability, responsiveness, or scalability in production React applications.",
