    deepExplanation: "An effect race occurs when multiple asynchronous operations overlap and an older operation finishes after a newer one, potentially overwriting current UI state. The solution is to make obsolete work unable to commit, commonly by aborting the request or tracking an operation identity.\\n\\n```tsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const controller = new AbortController();\n\n  setLoading(true);\n  fetch(`/api/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}`, {\n    signal: controller.signal,\n  })\n    .then(r => r.json())\n    .then(result => setResults(result))\n    .catch(error => {\n      if (error.name !== \"AbortError\") setError(error);\n    })\n    .finally(() => setLoading(false));\n\n  return () => controller.abort();\n}, [query]);\n```\\n\\nWhy it matters: This topic affects correctness, maintainability, responsiveness, or scalability in production React applications.",
    productionExample: "```tsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const controller = new AbortController();\n\n  setLoading(true);\n  fetch(`/api/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}`, {\n    signal: controller.signal,\n  })\n    .then(r => r.json())\n    .then(result => setResults(result))\n    .catch(error => {\n      if (error.name !== \"AbortError\") setError(error);\n    })\n    .finally(() => setLoading(false));\n\n  return () => controller.abort();\n}, [query]);\n```",
